---
import Base from '~/layouts/Base.astro';
import Card from '~/components/Card.astro';
---

<Base title="about" breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'about' }]}>
  <section class="mb-10 max-w-3xl">
    <div class="font-mono text-[10px] text-muted uppercase tracking-widest mb-2">about</div>
    <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold tracking-tight leading-tight mb-4">
      Cartograph is the notebook that <span class="text-accent">carries understanding forward</span>.
    </h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-muted leading-relaxed">
      Eight large open-source codebases — <code>jax</code>, <code>xla</code>,
      <code>orbax</code>, <code>tunix</code>, <code>tokamax</code>,
      <code>sglang</code>, <code>maxtext</code>, <code>qwix</code> — and one
      solo developer working across them through Claude. The bottleneck isn't file
      access; Claude already has fast tools. The bottleneck is that
      <em>nothing persists between sessions</em>. Every Monday's exploration
      vanishes by Friday. Every "I figured this out" insight has to be
      re-discovered.
    </p>
    <p class="text-lg text-muted leading-relaxed mt-4">
      Cartograph is the persistence layer. A folder of curated markdown files,
      revised in place when understanding deepens. The system gets <em>better</em>
      over time because the notes are <em>sharpened</em>, not just appended to.
    </p>
  </section>

  <Card eyebrow="01 / shape" title="Three layers of knowledge">
    <p class="text-sm text-muted mb-4 max-w-3xl">
      Every note belongs to one of three layers, distinguished by how durable
      it is and how often it gets revised.
    </p>
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
      <div class="border-2 border-border p-4 min-w-0">
        <div class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-widest text-muted">layer 1</div>
        <div class="font-bold text-lg mb-2">bedrock</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted">
          <code class="break-all">guides/&lt;repo&gt;/&lcub;overview,architecture,conventions&rcub;.md</code>.
          Durable, rarely revised, high authority. The "you have to know this"
          content for each codebase.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="border-2 border-border p-4 min-w-0">
        <div class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-widest text-muted">layer 2</div>
        <div class="font-bold text-lg mb-2">topic notes</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted">
          <code class="break-all">guides/&lt;repo&gt;/topics/&lt;slug&gt;.md</code>. Per-subsystem deep dives.
          <strong>Revised in place</strong> when code contradicts them — this is the
          load-bearing mechanic.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="border-2 border-border p-4 min-w-0">
        <div class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-widest text-muted">layer 3</div>
        <div class="font-bold text-lg mb-2">episodes</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted">
          <code class="break-all">episodes/YYYY-MM/&lt;slug&gt;.md</code>. Per-session insights, 200-600
          words each. Append-only; mark superseded when distilled into a topic.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-4 max-w-3xl">
      Flow upward: <strong>episodes → topic notes → bedrock</strong>. Each layer up
      is more distilled, more authoritative, more durable. Around the three
      core layers sit the supporting layers — <code>research/</code> (external
      comparisons), <code>papers/</code> (digested external work),
      <code>proposals/</code> (ambitious build plans), <code>designs/</code>
      (per-feature design docs), <code>setups/</code> (runnable harnesses), and
      <code>learn/</code> (ramp-ups + walkthroughs + drafts).
    </p>

    <div class="mt-6 max-w-3xl border-2 border-[var(--border-soft)] p-4">
      <div class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-widest text-muted">supporting layer</div>
      <div class="font-bold text-lg mb-2">proposals</div>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted mb-2">
        <code class="break-all">proposals/&lt;repo&gt;/&lt;slug&gt;/</code>. Ambitious,
        trend-justified build plans — each anchored by a falsifiable
        <em>investment thesis</em> (why it's worth building, why now). Synthesizes
        gap analysis, paper analysis, and ecosystem trend reads into a concrete
        thing to build.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted mb-2">
        Proposals are organized as <strong>trees</strong>: an umbrella proposal
        with nested sub-proposals beneath it, so a large theme decomposes into
        the pieces that ship it.
      </p>
      <div class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-widest text-muted mt-3 mb-1">Status lifecycle</div>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted">
        Each node moves through a pipeline:
        <code>gap-analysis</code> → <code>deep-dive</code> → <code>final</code> →
        <code>proposal-docx</code> → <code>design-docx</code> →
        <code>implementing</code>. A proposal that survives to <code>final</code>
        earns a formal proposal document, then a design document, then becomes
        a build.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Card>

  <Card eyebrow="02 / chassis" title="The automation chassis (what fires by itself)">
    <p class="text-sm text-muted mb-4 max-w-3xl">
      Cartograph compounds because the chassis runs unattended. Five hooks
      fire at specific moments in every Claude Code session under
      <code><cartograph-root>/</code>:
    </p>
    <ul class="space-y-3 max-w-3xl">
      <li class="border-2 border-[var(--border-soft)] p-3">
        <div class="font-mono text-xs font-bold mb-1">SessionStart</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted">
          Fetches upstream, opens drift reports if bedrock fell behind, rebuilds
          the file-reverse-index + BM25 index, writes today's diary entry,
          auto-promotes any tag with ≥3 undistilled episodes into a topic note,
          and enqueues pending notes for capped auto-review.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="border-2 border-[var(--border-soft)] p-3">
        <div class="font-mono text-xs font-bold mb-1">UserPromptSubmit</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted">
          On every turn: injects bedrock in full, then a <strong>lean menu</strong>
          per layer — the single best topic / episode / research match in full
          plus title-and-summary menus of the next seven, ranked by
          IDF-weighted keyword overlap with a usage boost (notes proven useful
          rise; notes surfaced five times and never followed sink). All layers
          dedup against each other, BM25 reranks what keywords missed, and the
          file-reverse lookup fires on any path-shaped token. The agent
          receives orientation before generating, at a fraction of the old
          token cost.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="border-2 border-[var(--border-soft)] p-3">
        <div class="font-mono text-xs font-bold mb-1">PreToolUse:Read</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted">
          When the agent is about to <code>Read</code> a workspace file, looks
          up the path in the reverse index and inserts every matching note as
          context. The orientation question — "what do we know about this file?"
          — is answered automatically.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="border-2 border-[var(--border-soft)] p-3">
        <div class="font-mono text-xs font-bold mb-1">Stop</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted">
          Auto-drafts an episode if the session touched ≥3 files but wrote
          none. Audits chassis utilization and attributes usage back to every
          injected note — a note counts as <em>used</em> when the session Read
          it or a file it cites, and that record feeds the next session's
          ranking. Commits + pushes any content the session wrote.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="border-2 border-[var(--border-soft)] p-3">
        <div class="font-mono text-xs font-bold mb-1">PostToolUse:Edit/Write</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted">
          Scans the diff for identity tokens (Cartograph / Claude / Anthropic)
          that must never reach upstream PRs. Appends to the session log.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Card>

  <Card eyebrow="02b / auto-promotion" title="The two cascades that make the layers compound">
    <p class="text-sm text-muted mb-4 max-w-3xl">
      <code>scripts/auto-promote.sh</code> runs in the background from
      <code>SessionStart</code> (155 lines, bash 3.2-compatible — macOS default).
      Two cascades, both <em>default-approve</em>: eligible work fires automatically;
      the human <em>rejects</em> to halt, doesn't <em>approve</em> to start.
      Opt-out entirely with <code>CARTOGRAPH_AUTO_PROMOTE=0</code>.
    </p>

    <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 max-w-5xl">
      <div class="border-2 border-[var(--border-soft)] p-4">
        <div class="font-mono text-xs font-bold mb-1">Cascade 1 — episode → topic</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted mb-3">
          Scans every <code>episodes/YYYY-MM/*.md</code>. Groups by
          <code>(repo, tag)</code>. Any tag with ≥<code>N</code> qualifying
          episodes (<code>N = CARTOGRAPH_AUTO_PROMOTE_EPISODES</code>, default
          <code>3</code>) becomes a candidate.
        </p>
        <div class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-widest text-muted mt-3 mb-1">An episode is eligible if</div>
        <ul class="text-sm text-muted list-disc pl-5 space-y-0.5">
          <li><code>distilled_into:</code> is unset (null / <code>~</code>)</li>
          <li><code>rejected: true</code> is NOT set</li>
          <li>has a <code>repo:</code> field</li>
          <li>has at least one tag (inline <code>tags: [a, b]</code> or block <code>- a</code> form)</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-widest text-muted mt-3 mb-1">An episode contributes to <em>every</em> tag it carries</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted">
          An episode tagged <code>tags: [orbax, jax]</code> counts toward both
          buckets. If <code>orbax</code> hits 3 first, that fires; <code>jax</code>
          might or might not depending on how many other <code>jax</code>-tagged
          episodes share the bucket.
        </p>
        <div class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-widest text-muted mt-3 mb-1">When it fires</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted">
          <code>nohup bash scripts/promote-tag.sh &lt;tag&gt; &amp;</code> — drafts
          <code>guides/&lt;repo&gt;/topics/&lt;tag&gt;.md</code> with
          <code>auto_promoted: true</code>, then stamps
          <code>distilled_into: guides/&lt;repo&gt;/topics/&lt;tag&gt;.md</code>
          on each source episode (which is how the audit knows not to re-promote
          on the next run).
        </p>
        <div class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-widest text-muted mt-3 mb-1">Skipped if</div>
        <ul class="text-sm text-muted list-disc pl-5 space-y-0.5">
          <li>topic file already exists AND ≥<code>count - 1</code> of the source episodes are already distilled into it (idempotency check)</li>
          <li>a stale lock file exists at <code>.auto-promote-locks/episode-to-topic-&lt;repo&gt;-&lt;tag&gt;.lock</code> (auto-reaped at &gt;1h)</li>
          <li><code>max_per_run</code> already promoted this run (default 2 — see <code>CARTOGRAPH_AUTO_PROMOTE_MAX_PER_RUN</code>)</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="border-2 border-[var(--border-soft)] p-4">
        <div class="font-mono text-xs font-bold mb-1">Cascade 2 — topic → bedrock</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted mb-3">
          Scans every <code>guides/&lt;repo&gt;/topics/*.md</code>. For each, fires
          <code>scripts/fold-topic-to-bedrock.sh &lt;repo&gt; &lt;name&gt;</code> —
          claude rewrites the relevant section of
          <code>overview.md</code> / <code>architecture.md</code> /
          <code>conventions.md</code> with the topic's content, then stamps
          <code>folded_into_bedrock:</code> on the topic.
        </p>
        <div class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-widest text-muted mt-3 mb-1">A topic is eligible if</div>
        <ul class="text-sm text-muted list-disc pl-5 space-y-0.5">
          <li><code>folded_into_bedrock:</code> is unset (null / <code>~</code>)</li>
          <li><code>rejected: true</code> is NOT set</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-widest text-muted mt-3 mb-1">Default-approve, not opt-in</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted">
          An earlier version of this required <code>reviewed_by_human:</code>
          before folding. That made bedrock fold opt-IN, which contradicts the
          cartograph-compounds-automatically property. Now: drafted topic →
          fold cascade fires next SessionStart unless you reject it. You
          actively <em>stop</em> the cascade; you don't <em>start</em> it.
        </p>
        <div class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-widest text-muted mt-3 mb-1">Skipped if</div>
        <ul class="text-sm text-muted list-disc pl-5 space-y-0.5">
          <li>lock file exists at <code>.auto-promote-locks/topic-to-bedrock-&lt;repo&gt;-&lt;name&gt;.lock</code></li>
          <li><code>max_per_run</code> reached</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-6 max-w-3xl">
      <div class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-muted mb-2">Worked example</div>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted mb-2">Say you finish three orbax sessions this week. Each writes an episode:</p>
      <ul class="font-mono text-xs text-muted list-none space-y-1 mb-3 pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--border-soft)]">
        <li>episodes/2026-05/2026-05-25-153140-cartograph.md · repo: orbax · tags: [orbax, layout-classes]</li>
        <li>episodes/2026-05/2026-05-25-153330-cartograph.md · repo: orbax · tags: [orbax, partial-load]</li>
        <li>episodes/2026-05/2026-05-25-173127-cartograph.md · repo: orbax · tags: [orbax, drift-resolution]</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted mb-2">
        Three episodes share the tag <code>orbax</code>. Each unique secondary tag
        (<code>layout-classes</code>, <code>partial-load</code>, <code>drift-resolution</code>)
        has count=1 — below threshold.
      </p>
      <ul class="text-sm text-muted list-disc pl-5 space-y-1 mb-3">
        <li><strong>Next SessionStart:</strong> <code>auto-promote.sh</code> finds the <code>(orbax, orbax)</code> bucket at count 3 → fires <code>promote-tag.sh orbax</code> in background.</li>
        <li><strong>~30–90s later:</strong> <code>guides/orbax/topics/orbax.md</code> exists with <code>auto_promoted: true</code>. Each source episode has <code>distilled_into: guides/orbax/topics/orbax.md</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Same SessionStart:</strong> the new topic file isn't seen by Cascade 2 yet (scan happened before the file existed) — fold waits one cycle.</li>
        <li><strong>Following SessionStart:</strong> Cascade 2 finds the new topic, fires <code>fold-topic-to-bedrock.sh orbax orbax</code> → claude weaves the topic's content into <code>guides/orbax/overview.md</code>, stamps <code>folded_into_bedrock: guides/orbax/overview.md</code> on the topic.</li>
        <li><strong>To halt at any point:</strong> set <code>rejected: true</code> on the episode (blocks promotion), or on the topic (blocks fold), or on the auto-drafted topic (blocks fold + flags for cleanup).</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-6 max-w-3xl">
      <div class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-muted mb-2">Manual overrides</div>
      <ul class="text-sm text-muted list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
        <li><a href="/promotions/" class="text-accent hover:underline">/promotions/</a> — the activity log + pending candidates. Manual <em>Promote → topic</em> button on each candidate row for cases where the auto-hook didn't fire (workspace-fork session, stuck lock, you crossed ≥3 mid-session and don't want to wait).</li>
        <li><code>/promote &lt;tag&gt;</code> slash — explicit one-off from within a Claude Code session.</li>
        <li>Edit <code>CARTOGRAPH_AUTO_PROMOTE_EPISODES</code> in your shell env to raise/lower the threshold per-session.</li>
        <li><code>CARTOGRAPH_AUTO_PROMOTE=0</code> opts out of both cascades for that session.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-6 max-w-3xl">
      <div class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-muted mb-2">Why default-approve</div>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted">
        The whole point of cartograph is that understanding compounds without
        being asked. If promotion required explicit approval, every session
        would carry a manual review tax — and most sessions you just want to
        keep working. Default-approve flips the polarity: claude does the
        drafting; the human only intervenes when the draft is wrong. Reject
        is cheap (one frontmatter field), so the asymmetry is fine.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Card>

  <Card eyebrow="02c / self-measurement" title="The loops that prove it works (and catch it failing)">
    <p class="text-sm text-muted mb-4 max-w-3xl">
      A knowledge system that only accumulates is a filing cabinet. These four
      mechanisms make the claims falsifiable and the failures visible:
    </p>
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 max-w-5xl">
      <div class="border-2 border-[var(--border-soft)] p-4">
        <div class="font-mono text-xs font-bold mb-1">Eval harness — golden questions</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted">
          <code>scripts/eval/run-eval.sh</code> holds 27 architecture questions
          across the eight repos, each graded by regex groups. Every question
          runs as a headless session twice — orientation on vs
          <code>CARTOGRAPH_INJECT_DISABLE=1</code> (which disables every
          injection surface) — and the per-arm deltas in score, turns, and
          wall time are the regression gate for every retrieval change.
          Refusal-shaped results (usage limits, overloads) are auto-flagged
          and excluded. Results time-series under
          <code>.cartograph/eval/</code>. Baseline (2026-06): 27/27 pass on
          both arms in ~5–6 turns — the corpus answers either way; the A/B
          measures push vs pull, not corpus vs nothing.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="border-2 border-[var(--border-soft)] p-4">
        <div class="font-mono text-xs font-bold mb-1">Usage feedback</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted">
          Every injection is recorded; every follow-up Read is attributed.
          Notes that earn their place rank higher next time; notes injected
          five times and never followed take a ranking penalty. Per-session
          <code>&lcub;injected, used&rcub;</code> records land in
          <code>usage-log.jsonl</code> for offline analysis.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="border-2 border-[var(--border-soft)] p-4">
        <div class="font-mono text-xs font-bold mb-1">Central error feed</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted">
          Chassis failures that used to vanish into <code>|| true</code> now
          append to <code>.cartograph/errors.log</code> and surface on the
          <a href="/console/" class="text-accent hover:underline">console</a>.
          Curation drains account per task — a failed task stays queued and
          retries instead of being silently dropped with its batch.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="border-2 border-[var(--border-soft)] p-4">
        <div class="font-mono text-xs font-bold mb-1">Auto-review + bloat control</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted">
          Notes awaiting review are judged in nightly batches by ONE capped
          headless agent (≤12/scan) that spot-checks anchors against the
          code. Approves leave the review queue silently; confident defects
          get <code>rejected: true</code>; near-duplicate episodes get
          <code>superseded_by</code> — and retired notes drop out of BM25 +
          injection menus (files are never deleted). Only contested calls
          reach the human, pre-annotated with verdict + reason. Editing a
          note voids its opinion and re-enters review.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="border-2 border-[var(--border-soft)] p-4">
        <div class="font-mono text-xs font-bold mb-1">Drift auto-fix + nightly maintenance</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted">
          The server closes drift on its own: a background loop resolves
          open drift reports every 30 minutes (repo-level via auto-revise,
          per-topic via the per-citation fixer), one capped headless agent
          at a time — pause with <code>CARTOGRAPH_DRIFT_AUTOFIX=0</code>.
          <code>scripts/setup-launchd.sh</code> adds KeepAlive supervision
          and a 03:30 maintenance backstop: the same drift drain, content
          lint, anchor-gap fixes, curation drain, session archival.
          Supervision is drift-proof: every server start routes through
          launchd (no detached process can win the port and lock the
          supervisor out), and a self-healer runs at SessionStart and in
          maintenance to clear any orphan and re-bind launchd if it ever
          does. The injection-cost card on
          <a href="/" class="text-accent hover:underline">Home</a> flags any
          repo whose bedrock outgrows its token budget.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Card>

  <Card eyebrow="03 / surfaces for humans" title="The local UI you're using right now">
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-3 max-w-4xl">
      <div class="border-2 border-[var(--border-soft)] p-3">
        <a href="/" class="font-bold text-accent hover:underline">Home <code class="text-xs">/</code></a>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-1">Live queue, file-index lookup, repo status, pinned notes, upstream activity. The first place you land.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="border-2 border-[var(--border-soft)] p-3">
        <a href="/prs/" class="font-bold text-accent hover:underline">PRs <code class="text-xs">/prs/</code></a>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-1">Every pull request opened by <code>[your-github-user]</code> across the eight tracked upstreams. State, mergeable, review decision, age.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="border-2 border-[var(--border-soft)] p-3">
        <a href="/repo/" class="font-bold text-accent hover:underline">Repos <code class="text-xs">/repo/</code></a>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-1">Pick a fork to dive into. Each repo has its own page with bedrock, topics, designs, research, papers, learn, episodes, drift, and stack.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="border-2 border-[var(--border-soft)] p-3">
        <a href="/repo/orbax/stack/" class="font-bold text-accent hover:underline">Stack tracker <code class="text-xs">/repo/&lt;r&gt;/stack/</code></a>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-1">Live tree of every local branch with PR state and cascade hints. Backed by ancestry walks; git-spice integration if installed.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="border-2 border-[var(--border-soft)] p-3">
        <a href="/episodes/" class="font-bold text-accent hover:underline">Episodes <code class="text-xs">/episodes/</code></a>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-1">Chronological timeline of every per-session insight. Filter by repo and tag.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="border-2 border-[var(--border-soft)] p-3">
        <a href="/library/" class="font-bold text-accent hover:underline">Library <code class="text-xs">/library/</code></a>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-1">Faceted index over designs, papers, research, walkthroughs, drafts, ramp-ups. Filter by type + repo.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="border-2 border-[var(--border-soft)] p-3">
        <a href="/proposals/" class="font-bold text-accent hover:underline">Proposals <code class="text-xs">/proposals/</code></a>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-1">The build-plan trees — umbrella proposals with nested sub-proposals, each at its stage in the gap-analysis → final → design lifecycle.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="border-2 border-[var(--border-soft)] p-3">
        <a href="/seams/" class="font-bold text-accent hover:underline">Seams <code class="text-xs">/seams/</code></a>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-1">Every cross-repo edge — jax↔xla, jax↔orbax, jax↔tunix — with the file paths and concepts that cross.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="border-2 border-[var(--border-soft)] p-3">
        <a href="/promotions/" class="font-bold text-accent hover:underline">Promotions <code class="text-xs">/promotions/</code></a>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-1">The promotion activity log + pending episode→topic candidates, with a manual Promote button for cases the auto-cascade didn't fire.</p>
